ing in cramped zoo  conditions meant that poor Dumbo  suffered these intense pains during his  relatively short life  eventually the Jews sold him to PT  Barnum for use in the circus in recent  times the bones of jumbo have been  examined and revealed how the  of stress had damaged him jumbo was only  24 years old when he passed away but his  bones showed painful wear and tear that  would be expected from an elephant twice  his age and if you think about it we see  the same type of thing happening with  the animated elephants in the movie  Dumbo particularly with mrs. jumbo she's  separated from her baby punished for  displaying normal elephant behaviour and  kept in cramped conditions sadly that's  actually an incredibly accurate  depiction of what life is like for the  vast majority of elephants in captivity  the real jumbo started out as a docile  elephant but soon became prone to  violent rages particularly in the  evening  this is the reason why the two  eventually considered him a liability  and sold him to the circus e-stat if the  London Zoo lacked the resources to care  for jumbo properly it's pretty baffling  to expect that a circus would according  to witnesses during the day  jumbo seemed to calm and content when  the Sun went down it was a different  story he would become possessed with  violent rages and destroy anything that  was in his path during these fits he  would even harm himself and one point he  broke off both of his husks when they  grew back he would use the stone of his  enclosure to grind them back down it got  so bad that his keeper Matthew Scott  resorted to giving him whiskey to try to  calm him down obviously that's an  unorthodox approach and something we  also saw happen in the movie Dumbo yes  that was clearly made in a different  time when it was okay to show baby  animals getting intoxicated in  movie let's consider the fact that a  fictional baby jumbo is so distraught at  being separated from his mother that he  sobs hysterically and then accidentally  gets drunk  would you mother think of you if she saw  you crying like this but with the  real-life jumbo his rages were written  off with the excuse that he was going  through maturity it's not unusual for  male elephants to become aggressive  during this time however modern  researchers disagree and add that if  this was the case  jumbo would have also become aggressive  to his keepers but this was not the case  current scientists who have examined his  remains for these there is a sad with  simple solution to his nighttime rages  elephants teeth grow and fall out  constantly but if their teeth don't wear  down properly they won't fall out  naturally this means that the new tooth  will be blocked causing a terrible  amount of pain in addition to not being  fed properly jumbo has fed tons of  sweets and snacks by well-meaning  patrons without the distractions  provided by daylight hours poor jumbo is  left alone with an excruciating pain in  his mouth for which he found no release  oh and those fun circus trains that we  see in the movie Dumbo the real jumbo  met his end when he was struck by a  locomotive the lives of circus elephants  are undeniably tragic but that's true  for many of the human performers as well  the history of circuses is long and  storied and filled with gross violations  of human rights you've probably heard of  people selling their kids to the circus  and that's actually a thing that  happened frequently people with  disabilities or medical problems would  be sold to circuses and forced to  perform for crowds of spectators gawking  at people who look different from us is  something that's generally frowned upon  in modern society but for a shockingly  long period of human history it was  perfectly acceptable freak shows were as  profitable as they were degrading and  for the most part their living  conditions were deplorable and let's not  forget perhaps the most uncomfortable  part of Dumbo the crows most children  